# The CONCEPT DOI, which always resolves to the newest release. The
# per-version DOI (10.5281/zenodo.22211591 for v0.3.1) is minted by Zenodo
# at each GitHub release and is recorded in README's Citing section; it
# must not sit here, where it would name an older release than `version`.
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22068632
repository-code: "https://github.com/mojolearn/mojolearn"
abstract: >-
  A GPU machine-learning library written in Mojo, and the first one to give
  the same source three vendors and the same BITS across them. Its
  distinguishing result is a three-tier numeric contract selected at runtime,
  where the strictest tier produces bit-identical answers on Apple Silicon
  via Metal, NVIDIA via CUDA and AMD via ROCm, verified by per-stage identity
  cards diffed across the three at pinned commits. Nothing upstream of this
  library has such a tier, because none of the CUDA implementations it draws
  on was ever asked to run anywhere but CUDA.
  ORIGINAL TO THIS WORK, with no upstream counterpart: the determinism
  ladder and its pinned-arithmetic primitives; the Metal backend; the
  host-side control plane every estimator is reached through; the identity
  cards, the host oracles and the sabotage methodology that gates them; the
  bit-identical FP32 GEMM contract; and the transformer and Mamba
  identity work. BY LINE COUNT, 84.9% of the Mojo in this repository lies
  outside the `ported/` directories and has no upstream file it corresponds
  to; the remaining 15.1% is the algorithmic substrate described next.
  THE SUBSTRATE IS DERIVED WORK AND IS DOCUMENTED AS SUCH, per file, with
  the upstream path and pinned commit, in the PORTED_MAP tables and in each
  file's own header: gradient-boosted oblivious (symmetric) trees from
  CatBoost; random forests, extremely randomized trees, DBSCAN, PCA, ridge
  and logistic regression, coordinate descent, support vector
  classification, kernel density estimation, single-linkage and spectral
  clustering, isolation forests, Holt-Winters and the scoring metrics from
  cuML; k-means and brute-force k-nearest-neighbors from cuVS; and ordinary
  least squares, top-k selection and the MST and Lanczos primitives from
  RAFT. Those algorithms are the upstreams' work. See NOTICE for the license
  obligations each carries, including the MIT-licensed FAISS warp-select
  queue that RAFT vendors.
keywords:
  - machine learning
  - GPU
  - Metal
  - Apple Silicon
  - Mojo
  - clustering
  - k-means
  - DBSCAN
  - nearest neighbors
  - PCA
  - gradient boosting
  - oblivious trees
